2. Causal Stochastic Differential Equations and the do‑Operator

2.1 Probabilistic setting and causal parametrisation

Let (\Omega,\mathcal{F},\{\mathcal{F}_t\}_{t\ge 0},\mathbb{P}) be a filtered probability space satisfying the usual conditions. The physical state x_t takes values in an open connected set \mathcal{X}\subset\mathbb{R}^n. The instantaneous causal structure is encoded by a directed acyclic graph G = (V,E) with |V| = n, represented by its adjacency matrix A \in \{0,1\}^{n\times n}, where A_{ij}=1 iff the edge i \to j is present. The set of admissible DAGs is a finite collection \mathcal{G}.

Definition 2.1 (C‑SDE). The state evolves according to

\mathrm{d}x_t = f(x_t, A)\,\mathrm{d}t + \sigma(x_t, A)\,\mathrm{d}W_t, \qquad x_0 = x^0, \tag{1}

where W_t is an m-dimensional standard Brownian motion. The coefficients satisfy:

· (Local Lipschitz) For each A\in\mathcal{G}, f(\cdot,A) and \sigma(\cdot,A) are locally Lipschitz in x, uniformly over \mathcal{G}.
· (Linear growth) \|f(x,A)\| + \|\sigma(x,A)\| \le K(1+\|x\|) for all x,A.
· (Causal sparsity) The j-th component of f and the j-th row of \sigma depend only on x_j and those x_i with A_{ij}=1:
  f_j(x,A) = f_j(x_j, x_{\mathrm{Pa}_A(j)}), \quad
  \sigma_{j\cdot}(x,A) = \sigma_{j\cdot}(x_j, x_{\mathrm{Pa}_A(j)}).

2.2 The structural do‑operator

An intervention u is a subset of edges to be deleted from the current graph A. The mapping

\mathrm{do}(u) : A \mapsto A' = A \odot ( \mathbf{1}\mathbf{1}^\top - M(u) ),

where M(u) \in \{0,1\}^{n\times n} with M_{ij}=1 iff the edge i\to j is pruned, and \odot is elementwise product. The set \mathcal{U} of admissible interventions comprises all edge subsets such that A' \in \mathcal{G}. The post‑intervention dynamics are instantaneously governed by (1) with graph A'. Interventions are thus monotonic edge removals; they never introduce new dependencies, preserving acyclicity.

Remark 2.1 (Causal interpretation). This operation directly mirrors the do‑operator in Pearl’s structural causal models (SCM). In the SCM setting, an intervention on a variable corresponds to removing incoming edges in the causal diagram and fixing a conditional distribution. Here, pruning an edge i\to j in the SDE means that the differential of x_j no longer depends on x_i; the functional dependence is severed. This preserves the modularity of the causal model and enables the use of causal identifiability results for dynamical systems. For a given post‑intervention graph A', the risk functional \mathcal{R}_{A'} can, in principle, be estimated from observational data under the original graph using the do‑calculus adapted to SDEs (see e.g. Bongers & Mooij, 2018). Full exploitation of this link is deferred to future work, but the structural similarity guarantees that our control actions have a clean causal semantics.

