3. Causal Risk Functional and Policy‑Independent Barrier

3.1 First‑passage risk and Feynman–Kac

Let \mathcal{F} \subset \mathcal{X} be a closed set (catastrophic failure), a smooth manifold with boundary. The system starts in \mathcal{X}\setminus\mathcal{F}.

Definition 3.1 (Causal first‑passage time). For a post‑intervention graph A and initial state x\notin\mathcal{F},

\tau_{\mathcal{F}}^{A} = \inf\{ t \ge 0 : x_t \in \mathcal{F} \mid x_0 = x,\; \text{dynamics }(1)\text{ with graph }A \}.

Definition 3.2 (Causal risk functional). With discount \lambda > 0,

\mathcal{R}_A(x) = \mathbb{E}_x\!\big[ e^{-\lambda \tau_{\mathcal{F}}^{A}} \big], \qquad \mathcal{R}_A|_{\partial\mathcal{F}} = 1. \tag{2}

Theorem 3.1 (Feynman–Kac). Assume f,\sigma are C^2 in x with bounded derivatives and \sigma\sigma^\top is uniformly elliptic on \mathcal{X}\setminus\mathcal{F}. Then \mathcal{R}_A is the unique bounded classical solution of

\mathcal{A}_A \mathcal{R}_A - \lambda \mathcal{R}_A = 0 \quad \text{in } \mathcal{X}\setminus\mathcal{F}, \qquad \mathcal{R}_A = 1 \text{ on } \partial\mathcal{F}, \tag{3}

where \mathcal{A}_A is the infinitesimal generator of (1) for graph A:

\mathcal{A}_A \phi = f(x,A)\cdot\nabla\phi + \tfrac12 \operatorname{tr}\!\big( \sigma(x,A)\sigma(x,A)^\top \nabla^2\phi \big).

Proof. Standard Feynman–Kac for elliptic operators with absorption; see Øksendal (2003). ∎

Remark 3.1 (Degenerate noise). The assumption of uniform ellipticity ensures the existence of a classical solution. In many causal SDEs, the noise may be degenerate (some state components have zero diffusion). In such cases, one may resort to viscosity solutions or subelliptic PDE theory. The viability theorems below can be adapted to such settings as long as the risk functional is continuous and the associated stochastic process reaches the failure set with positive probability under the wrong graph. This extension is an important direction for future work.

3.2 Admissible interventions and impulse cost

Each intervention consumes resources \Phi(u) = (E, T, B, L)^\top (e.g., energy, time, bandwidth, computational load) with hard capacity limits M\in\mathbb{R}^4_{>0}. The admissible set at state x is

\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(x) = \{ u \in \mathcal{U} : \Phi_i(u) < M_i,\ i=1,\dots,4 \}. \tag{4}

Interventions are executed at stopping times \{\tau_k\}_{k\ge 1}. At each \tau_k an action u_k \in \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(x_{\tau_k}) is selected, the graph switches from A_{k-1} to A_k = \mathrm{do}(u_k)[A_{k-1}], and an impulse cost

C(u) = -\sum_{i=1}^4 \log\!\Big(1 - \frac{\Phi_i(u)}{M_i}\Big) \tag{5}

is incurred. The cost tends to +\infty as any resource nears capacity. Note that (5) does not directly account for the benefit of the intervention—it only measures resource expenditure. In practice, a control policy will select among admissible interventions the one that minimally satisfies the safety condition (14) with the smallest cost, effectively implementing a minimal‑cost safe controller.

3.3 Policy‑independent causal barrier

To avoid defining safety through an optimal policy, we introduce the worst‑case admissible risk

\rho(x, A) = \sup_{u \in \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(x)} \mathcal{R}_{A'}(x), \qquad A' = \mathrm{do}(u)[A]. \tag{6}

The causal control barrier function (CCBF) is

h(x, A) = \varepsilon - \rho(x, A), \qquad \varepsilon > 0. \tag{7}

The physical safe region for graph A is \mathcal{S}_A = \{ x : h(x,A) \ge 0 \}. Because \rho depends only on the set of admissible controls and the current graph, h is policy‑independent.

Remark 3.2 (Existence of admissible interventions). The definition of \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(x) requires that at every encountered state there is at least one feasible intervention (Assumption A1). In general, this is a reachability condition that may be hard to verify a priori. For practical design, we may ensure that a trivial intervention—switching to a pre‑computed safe fallback graph A_{\mathrm{safe}} (see Section 7)—is always available, even if it does not reduce the risk below \varepsilon. The existence of such a fallback can be checked offline, and the safety margin \varepsilon can be chosen large enough to absorb the transient.

