4. Meta‑Learning and Global Viability Index

4.1 Meta‑learning as a log‑submartingale

The system continuously updates internal models to identify the true causal graph after a disturbance. This meta‑learning process is modelled by a jump‑diffusion for parameters \theta_t \in \Theta \subset \mathbb{R}^d:

\mathrm{d}\theta_t = a(\theta_t)\,\mathrm{d}t + b(\theta_t)\,\mathrm{d}W^\theta_t + \int_{\mathbb{R}^q} c(\theta_{t-},z)\,\widetilde{N}(\mathrm{d}t,\mathrm{d}z), \tag{8}

where W^\theta is an r-dimensional Brownian motion (possibly correlated with W) and \widetilde{N} is a compensated Poisson random measure with Lévy measure \nu. Standard Lipschitz and growth conditions hold.

Let V: \Theta \to [1,\infty) be a cognitive stability functional. We impose directly that \ln V is a local sub‑martingale:

\mathcal{A}^\theta_c (\ln V)(\theta) \ge 0, \qquad
\int_{\mathbb{R}^q} \big[ \ln V(\theta + c(\theta,z)) - \ln V(\theta) \big] \nu(\mathrm{d}z) \ge 0, \tag{9}

where \mathcal{A}^\theta_c is the generator of the continuous part of (8).

Example 4.1 (Bayesian graph discovery). The true graph A^* is unknown among the finite set \mathcal{G}. The system maintains a posterior probability vector \theta_t \in \Delta^{|\mathcal{G}|-1} via Bayesian updating from continuous observations. Taking V(\theta) = 1/\theta_{i^*} (where i^* is the index of the true graph) yields \ln V_t = -\ln \theta_{i^*,t}. Because the posterior of the true graph is a martingale and -\ln is convex, \ln V_t is a submartingale, satisfying (9). The larger the uncertainty, the larger the negative drift of \ln V, penalising ignorance.

4.2 Global viability index

The interventions occur at stopping times \{\tau_k\}. Between interventions, the active graph A(t) is constant. The global viability index is

\mathcal{V}_t = h(x_t, A(t)) \cdot V(\theta_t) \cdot \exp\!\Big( -\sum_{\tau_k \le t} C(u_k) \Big). \tag{10}

Its logarithm

L_t = \ln h(x_t, A(t)) + \ln V(\theta_t) - \sum_{\tau_k \le t} C(u_k) \tag{11}

is the sum of a physical safety margin, a learning margin, and the negative accumulated cost. The safe envelope is \{\mathcal{V}_t \ge \mathcal{V}_{\min}\} with 0 < \mathcal{V}_{\min} < \varepsilon. At an intervention time \tau_k, the jump in L_t is

\Delta L_{\tau_k} = \ln h(x_{\tau_k}, A_k) - \ln h(x_{\tau_k}, A_{k-1}) - C(u_k). \tag{12}

