6. Viability Theorem via Stochastic Nagumo Condition

6.1 Stochastic hybrid system formulation

The overall system is a stochastic hybrid process: between discrete switches the state evolves according to an SDE with constant graph; at switching times the graph and the index jump according to the intervention. To apply known stochastic viability results we consider the continuous phases separately and then piece them together with the jump condition.

6.2 Tangential noise condition

Almost‑sure invariance of the set \{L \ge \ln\mathcal{V}_{\min}\} under a non‑degenerate diffusion is generally impossible unless the noise is purely tangent to the boundary. We therefore require an additional structural condition.

Condition 6.1 (Tangential noise on the boundary). On the set \{(x,\theta) : L(x,\theta,A) = \ln\mathcal{V}_{\min}\}, the gradient of L (with respect to (x,\theta)) is orthogonal to the noise coefficients, i.e.,

\nabla_x \ln h(x,A)^\top \sigma(x,A) = 0, \qquad
\nabla_\theta \ln V(\theta)^\top b(\theta) = 0,

and for the jump part, \ln V(\theta + c(\theta,z)) = \ln V(\theta) whenever L = \ln\mathcal{V}_{\min}.

This condition can be satisfied by design: for instance, by constructing h such that its gradient lies in the kernel of \sigma on the boundary, and by ensuring that the meta‑learning diffusion vanishes when the viability margin is minimal. In the Bayesian example, one may regularise the posterior update to become deterministic near the boundary.

6.3 Invariance theorem

Theorem 6.1 (Causal viability invariance). Let Assumptions (A1)–(A5) below hold and suppose the system starts with \mathcal{V}_{t_0} \ge \mathcal{V}_{\min} > 0 almost surely. Then

\mathbb{P}\Big( \mathcal{V}_t \ge \mathcal{V}_{\min},\ \forall t \ge t_0 \Big) = 1.

Consequently, h(x_t, A(t)) > 0 for all t, and the state never reaches \mathcal{F}.

Assumptions.

· (A1) (Admissibility) \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(x_t) \neq \emptyset for all t a.s.
· (A2) (Feasibility) There exists a control policy selecting interventions such that Conditions 5.1, 5.2, and 6.1 hold, and the resulting stochastic hybrid system has a unique strong solution.
· (A3) (Time‑scale separation – postponed to Section 7)
· (A4) (Log‑submartingale meta‑learning) Condition (9) holds.
· (A5) (Regularity) f,\sigma,a,b,c are Lipschitz with linear growth. The set \mathcal{X}\setminus\mathcal{F} is a C^2 domain.

Proof. We consider the process between interventions. Fix an interval where the active graph is A. The set K = \{ (x,\theta) : L(x,\theta,A) \ge \ln\mathcal{V}_{\min} \} is closed. On the boundary \partial K = \{L = \ln\mathcal{V}_{\min}\}, Condition 6.1 guarantees that the diffusion and jump terms are tangent to K. Condition 5.1 implies that the drift of L satisfies

\mathcal{A}^{\mathrm{tot}}_A L \ge -\alpha(L - \ln\mathcal{V}_{\min}) = 0 \quad \text{on } \partial K.

Thus, at every point of \partial K, the generator applied to a smooth defining function is non‑negative and the noise does not push outward. By the stochastic Nagumo theorem for jump‑diffusions (see, e.g., Da Prato and Frankowska, 2007; or Bujorianu and Lygeros, 2006, for hybrid systems), the set K is almost surely invariant under the dynamics (1),(8) with constant graph A. Hence L_t \ge \ln\mathcal{V}_{\min} throughout the interval.

At an intervention time \tau_k, Condition 5.2 ensures L_{\tau_k} \ge \ln\mathcal{V}_{\min}. Patching together the continuous intervals with the jumps, we conclude L_t \ge \ln\mathcal{V}_{\min} for all t. From \mathcal{V}_t = e^{L_t} \ge \mathcal{V}_{\min} > 0 and the definition \mathcal{V}_t = h V e^{-\sum C}, we obtain h = \mathcal{V}_t V^{-1} e^{\sum C} > 0 because all factors are finite and positive. Thus the physical barrier never vanishes, and by (7) the state cannot enter \mathcal{F}. ∎

6.4 Relaxing the tangential noise condition: high‑probability viability

The tangential noise condition (6.1) is restrictive—it essentially requires that the noise be “benign” exactly at the safety boundary. In many practical systems, this cannot be guaranteed. We may relax the conclusion to a high‑probability guarantee by employing the theory of stochastic control barrier functions (CBFs). Suppose that on the boundary \partial K we only have the drift condition (13) but the noise may point outward with a bounded intensity. Then one can use a martingale argument to bound the probability of leaving K within a finite time horizon. Specifically, if there exists a constant \kappa such that the quadratic variation of the noise component of L satisfies \mathrm{d}\langle L^{\mathrm{noise}}\rangle_t \le \kappa^2 \,\mathrm{d}t near the boundary, then for any \delta>0 and time horizon T,

\mathbb{P}\Big( \inf_{t\in[0,T]} L_t \le \ln\mathcal{V}_{\min} - \delta \Big) \le \exp\!\Big(-\frac{\delta^2}{2\kappa^2 T}\Big)

under a worst‑case analysis. This yields a practical, probabilistic safety certificate: the system stays safe with high probability if the safety margin \varepsilon (and thus \mathcal{V}_{\min}) is chosen sufficiently large relative to the noise intensity. The full development of such high‑probability viability for the ACRL framework is an immediate extension and will be detailed in a companion paper. This relaxation significantly widens the applicability of the approach, as only the drift condition (13) needs to be enforced by control, not the tangentiality of the noise.

