7. Time‑Scale Separation and Delay‑Robust Causal Identification

A disturbance may change the true causal graph. The meta‑learning process requires a maximal time \tau_G to re‑identify the correct graph with high confidence. During this blind interval, the system falls back to a known safe graph A_{\mathrm{safe}}, which is a subgraph of every possible true graph.

7.1 Perturbation bound for the risk functional

Lemma 7.1 (Risk increase under incorrect graph). Let the true risk functional for graph A^* satisfy \|\nabla \mathcal{R}_{A^*}\| \le K_R and let the generator difference be bounded as

| (\mathcal{A}_{A_{\mathrm{safe}}} - \mathcal{A}_{A^*}) \mathcal{R}_{A^*} | \le \eta \|A_{\mathrm{safe}} - A^*\|.

Then, starting from x_0 with initial risk r_0 = \mathcal{R}_{A^*}(x_0), after time s evolving under A_{\mathrm{safe}},

\mathbb{E}\big[ e^{-\lambda \tau_{\mathcal{F}}^{A^*}} \mid \mathcal{F}_s \big] \le e^{-\lambda s} r_0 + \frac{\eta}{\lambda}(1 - e^{-\lambda s}).

Proof. By Itô’s formula and the definition of the risk functional, e^{-\lambda t} \mathcal{R}_{A^*}(x_t) is a martingale under the true generator \mathcal{A}_{A^*}. Under \mathcal{A}_{A_{\mathrm{safe}}} it acquires a drift bounded by \eta e^{-\lambda t}. Integrating yields the bound. ∎

7.2 Viability margin under delay

Theorem 7.1 (Delay‑robustness). Assume that during the blind interval of length \tau_G the system uses the safe fallback A_{\mathrm{safe}} and that the meta‑learning process continues to satisfy (9). Let the viability index at the start of the blind period be L_{\mathrm{start}} \ge \ln\mathcal{V}_{\min} + \Delta with \Delta > 0. If the safety margin satisfies

\varepsilon \ge \mathcal{V}_{\min} \exp\!\big( \tau_G (c_0 + \eta) \big) (1 + \gamma),

where c_0 bounds the maximum rate of decrease of \ln V and \gamma is a constant from the risk bound, then L_t > \ln\mathcal{V}_{\min} for all t in the blind interval almost surely.

Proof sketch. The continuous viability drift (13) may fail under the fallback graph, but the worst‑case decay of L_t is bounded by a linear rate. Combining Lemma 7.1 with the log‑submartingale property of \ln V gives a lower bound on L_t after time \tau_G. The margin condition ensures it stays strictly positive. ∎

This theorem provides a concrete design rule: increasing the physical barrier \varepsilon compensates for slower graph identification. The trade‑off between topological complexity (which increases \tau_G, depending on the sample complexity of causal discovery) and safety margin is explicit.

