8. Illustrative Example: Linear Chain System

Consider a three‑variable linear chain: X_1 \to X_2 \to X_3. The state x = (x_1,x_2,x_3)^\top evolves as

\mathrm{d}x_t = -A x_t\,\mathrm{d}t + \Sigma\,\mathrm{d}W_t,

with A = \begin{pmatrix} a_{11} & 0 & 0 \\ a_{21} & a_{22} & 0 \\ 0 & a_{32} & a_{33} \end{pmatrix} when the full chain is active. The admissible DAGs are subgraphs obtained by deleting edges: full chain, X_1\to X_2 only, X_2\to X_3 only, and empty graph. The failure set is \mathcal{F} = \{ x : x_3 \ge B \}. Because the dynamics of x_3 decouple when a_{32}=0, the risk functional can be computed analytically from the scalar Ornstein‑Uhlenbeck process of x_3. We choose parameters: a_{11}=a_{22}=a_{33}=1, a_{21}=0.5, a_{32}=0.8, \Sigma = 0.2 I, B=5, \lambda=0.1. The worst‑case risk \rho(x,A) is evaluated over the admissible interventions. With \varepsilon = 0.4, a non‑empty safe set exists.

The meta‑learning process maintains a Bayesian posterior over the four graphs. The stability functional V is the inverse probability of the true graph, yielding the log‑submartingale property. The impulse cost C(u) penalises retaining edges. A policy that prunes X_2\to X_3 when x_3 exceeds a threshold satisfies the continuous viability condition with a linear \alpha. The tangential noise condition holds because on the boundary L = \ln\mathcal{V}_{\min} the gradient of \ln h is orthogonal to the noise acting on x_3 due to the decoupling. Numerical simulations confirm that the viability index stays above \mathcal{V}_{\min}=0.2, while an unswitched system violates the barrier.

