9. Discussion and Future Work

Relation to stochastic viability theory. Theorem 6.1 is an application of the stochastic Nagumo condition for jump‑diffusions, placing the framework on solid mathematical ground. The high‑probability relaxation outlined in Section 6.4 makes the method applicable even when noise cannot be made tangent; this aligns with the broader literature on stochastic control barrier functions and probabilistic safety.

Proactive topology design. The safety margin depends on graph complexity. The controller can deliberately simplify the causal structure to reduce identification delay \tau_G and metabolic cost, leading to a self‑aware resilience loop.

Identifiability and do‑calculus for SDEs. The connection to Pearl’s do‑calculus opens the door to leveraging causal discovery algorithms that can estimate post‑intervention risk from observational data, even under distributional shifts. The sample complexity of such algorithms directly influences \tau_G and hence the required safety margin.

Open problems. Existence of policies satisfying the unified conditions for general nonlinear systems, tight sample‑complexity bounds for \tau_G, extension to partially observed states, and learning the barrier function online are important future directions. The design of a fallback graph A_{\mathrm{safe}} that always satisfies A1 also warrants further study.

---

10. Conclusion

We have laid a rigorous foundation for structural causal resilience. By treating interventions as graph modifications, we bridged stochastic viability and causal inference. The policy‑independent barrier, log‑submartingale meta‑learning, and unified conditions yield an almost‑sure safety theorem proved via stochastic Nagumo conditions; the restrictive tangential noise requirement is then relaxed to a high‑probability guarantee, expanding the scope of the theory. The delay‑robustness theorem quantifies the safety margin needed for graph re‑identification. The framework, illustrated on a linear chain, opens a new line of inquiry at the intersection of control, causality, and machine learning.

Appendices :

Appendix A. Regularity of the Barrier Function

In Section 3, the causal barrier is defined as h(x,A)=\varepsilon-\rho(x,A) where

\rho(x,A)=\sup_{u\in\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(x)}\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{do}(u)[A]}(x).

Each \mathcal{R}_{A'} is, under the assumptions of Theorem 3.1, a classical C^{2} solution of the Feynman–Kac PDE. However, the pointwise supremum of a family of smooth functions is generally only locally Lipschitz, and not necessarily differentiable. Consequently expressions such as \mathcal{A}_A(\ln h) that appear in Condition 5.1 and in the viability analysis require justification.

We resolve this issue by adopting the viscosity solution framework, which is the natural setting for non‑smooth barrier functions in stochastic control. The following proposition shows that the viability argument is unaffected.

Proposition A.1 (Viscosity characterisation of the viability condition).
Assume that for each fixed graph A, the function h(\cdot,A) is continuous and that it satisfies, in the viscosity sense,

\mathcal{A}_A (\ln h)(x) + \mathcal{A}^\theta (\ln V)(\theta) \ge -\alpha(L - \ln\mathcal{V}_{\min})

on the set \{L>\ln\mathcal{V}_{\min}\}. Then the conclusions of Theorem 6.1 remain true, provided that on the boundary \partial K the tangential noise condition holds and the viscosity sub‑solution property guarantees the drift condition is satisfied at all points where \ln h is not differentiable.

Proof. The proof is a direct adaptation of the stochastic viability results for viscosity sub‑solutions (see, e.g., Buckdahn, Peng, Quincampoix & Rainer, 2001). The key point is that the invariance of the set K under the controlled jump‑diffusion can be deduced from the viscosity inequality together with the tangentiality of the noise, without requiring pointwise differentiability. ∎

For readers more comfortable with smooth approximations, one may also regularise \rho by a mollification parameter \delta>0, replacing \sup with a smooth soft‑maximum. The results then hold uniformly in \delta, and the limit \delta\to0 recovers the original barrier. All subsequent viability statements are to be understood in the viscosity sense whenever h is not C^{2}.

