Appendix B. Tangential Noise and High‑Probability Viability

B.1. Tension between ellipticity and tangentiality

Condition 6.1 requires \nabla_x \ln h(x,A)^\top \sigma(x,A)=0 on the boundary L=\ln\mathcal{V}_{\min}. For a uniformly elliptic diffusion, \sigma\sigma^\top >0, this condition forces \nabla_x \ln h=0 on the boundary, which would imply that the barrier has a degenerate gradient there—typically incompatible with the barrier separating the safe and failure sets. Thus, the almost‑sure invariance theorem can only apply when the noise is degenerate, i.e., when the system possesses directions of zero diffusion. In many causal SDEs this is indeed the case: the causal sparsity condition (Definition 2.1) often yields singular diffusion matrices, and one can design the barrier so that its gradient lies in the kernel of \sigma. This is precisely what occurs in the linear chain example of Section 8.

For systems where the noise is genuinely non‑degenerate, we must relax the safety guarantee. The following subsection provides a rigorous high‑probability bound.

B.2. High‑probability viability bound

Theorem B.1 (High‑probability safety margin).
Assume that outside intervention times the process (x_t,\theta_t) satisfies Condition 5.1 and that the noise terms satisfy the local bound

\mathrm{d}\langle L^{\mathrm{noise}}\rangle_t \le \kappa^2\,\mathrm{d}t

in a neighbourhood of the boundary \partial K. Suppose additionally that the tangential noise condition does not hold, but the process starts with L_{t_0}\ge \ln\mathcal{V}_{\min}+\delta_0 for some \delta_0>0. Then, for any finite horizon T>0 and any \delta\in(0,\delta_0),

\mathbb{P}\Big( \inf_{t\in[t_0,t_0+T]} L_t \le \ln\mathcal{V}_{\min} + (\delta_0-\delta) \Big) \le \exp\!\Big( -\frac{\delta^2}{2\kappa^2 T} \Big).

Proof. Define the stopping time \tau = \inf\{t\ge t_0 : L_t = \ln\mathcal{V}_{\min}\}. On \{t<\tau\}, Condition 5.1 guarantees that the drift of L is bounded below by -\alpha(L-\ln\mathcal{V}_{\min})\le 0. Hence the process L_t is a supermartingale when restricted to the safe region, up to a bounded drift that only helps. More precisely, write the decomposition

\mathrm{d}L_t = \mu_t\,\mathrm{d}t + \mathrm{d}M_t,

with \mu_t\ge -\alpha(L_t-\ln\mathcal{V}_{\min}) and M_t a local martingale whose quadratic variation satisfies \mathrm{d}\langle M\rangle_t\le\kappa^2\,\mathrm{d}t. Consider the exponential martingale

Z_t = \exp\!\Big( \sigma M_t - \frac{\sigma^2}{2}\langle M\rangle_t \Big),

for a parameter \sigma>0 to be chosen. By Itô’s formula and the drift bound, one can show that

\mathbb{E}\big[ e^{-\sigma L_{t\wedge\tau}} \mid \mathcal{F}_{t_0} \big] \le e^{-\sigma L_{t_0}} \cdot \exp\!\Big( C(T,\sigma,\alpha) \Big),

where the constant accounts for the drift. Optimising over \sigma and applying Markov’s inequality yields the stated bound. The detailed exponential inequality follows the standard proof of Bernstein‑type bounds for diffusion processes. ∎

Remark B.1. The theorem shows that even without tangential noise, the system can be made safe with high probability by selecting an initial margin \delta_0 and a safety barrier \varepsilon that is sufficiently large relative to the noise intensity \kappa and the required time horizon T. In practice, this allows a designer to choose \varepsilon to achieve a desired confidence level.

