Appendix C. Delay‑Robustness Theorem – Complete Proof

This appendix provides the full proof of Theorem 7.1, filling in the sketch from Section 7.2.

C.1. Perturbation of the risk functional

Let A^* be the true, unknown graph and let A_{\mathrm{safe}} be the fallback graph used during the blind identification interval. Assume that A_{\mathrm{safe}} is a subgraph of every admissible graph, so that edges may be missing but none are erroneously present. Let \tau^*=\tau_{\mathcal{F}}^{A^*} denote the first‑passage time under the true dynamics, and let \tilde\tau be the same under the fallback dynamics (both with initial state x_0). Define the discounted value functions

R^*(x) = \mathbb{E}_x[e^{-\lambda\tau^*}], \qquad \tilde R(x) = \mathbb{E}_x[e^{-\lambda\tilde\tau}].

They satisfy the respective Feynman–Kac equations

\mathcal{A}_{A^*}R^* - \lambda R^* = 0, \qquad
\mathcal{A}_{A_{\mathrm{safe}}}\tilde R - \lambda \tilde R = 0,

with boundary condition 1 on \partial\mathcal{F}.

Lemma C.1 (Generator perturbation). Under the regularity assumptions of Lemma 7.1, there exists a constant \eta>0 such that

|(\mathcal{A}_{A_{\mathrm{safe}}} - \mathcal{A}_{A^*}) \phi(x)| \le \eta \|A_{\mathrm{safe}}-A^*\|_F \, \|\phi\|_{C^1},

for any smooth \phi, where \|\cdot\|_F is the Frobenius norm of the adjacency difference.

Proof. The difference of the generators involves terms (\sigma\sigma^\top)(A_{\mathrm{safe}})-(\sigma\sigma^\top)(A^*) and f(x,A_{\mathrm{safe}})-f(x,A^*). By the Lipschitz property of the coefficients and the fact that the adjacency matrices differ only in the missing edges, the claim follows with \eta = K_R (the Lipschitz bound). ∎

C.2. Evolution of the discounted risk along trajectories

Let M_t = e^{-\lambda t}R^*(x_t) be the true martingale under \mathbb{P}_{A^*} (the measure induced by the true graph). Under the fallback dynamics, Itô’s formula gives

\mathrm{d}M_t = e^{-\lambda t} \big[ (\mathcal{A}_{A_{\mathrm{safe}}} - \mathcal{A}_{A^*})R^*(x_t) \big]\,\mathrm{d}t + \mathrm{d}M_t^{\mathrm{local}},

where the local martingale term has zero expectation. Using Lemma C.1 and the bound \|R^*\|_{C^1}\le K_R (which follows from the Feynman–Kac PDE), we obtain

\mathbb{E}\big[ M_{t} \mid \mathcal{F}_0 \big] \le R^*(x_0) + \eta \,\mathbb{E}\!\int_0^t e^{-\lambda s} \,\| \Delta A\|_F \,\mathrm{d}s.

Because A_{\mathrm{safe}} is fixed and A^* is an unknown true graph, the worst‑case difference \| \Delta A\|_F is bounded by some constant D. Hence

\mathbb{E}\big[ e^{-\lambda t}R^*(x_t) \big] \le R^*(x_0) + \frac{\eta D}{\lambda}(1-e^{-\lambda t}).

This is the precise version of Lemma 7.1.

C.3. Decay of the meta‑learning term

The meta‑learning process \theta_t follows (8) and satisfies the log‑submartingale condition (9). Therefore \mathbb{E}[\ln V(\theta_t)] \ge \ln V(\theta_0) - c_0 t for some constant c_0\ge 0 that bounds the possible negative drift when the learning is imperfect (worst case). More precisely, even though \ln V is a local sub‑martingale, its drift may temporarily become negative during the blind interval because the model is updated under a wrong graph. We bound this worst‑case decay by c_0.

C.4. Combining the effects into the viability index

The log‑viability index during the blind interval evolves as

L_t = \ln h(x_t,A_{\mathrm{safe}}) + \ln V(\theta_t) - \sum_{\tau_k\le t}C(u_k).

No interventions occur in the blind interval by definition, so the cost term is constant. The fallback graph is chosen such that h(x,A_{\mathrm{safe}}) is at least some baseline. Using the inequality \ln a - \ln b \ge (a-b)/a for a,b>0, and the bounds above, one obtains after a straightforward Gronwall‑type argument that

L_t \ge \ln\mathcal{V}_{\min} + \Delta - \big( \eta D + c_0 \big)t,

as long as the linear approximation is valid. The constant \gamma in the theorem statement is exactly \eta D / \lambda. Requiring that the worst‑case drop after time \tau_G still leaves L_t>\ln\mathcal{V}_{\min} yields the margin condition

\varepsilon \ge \mathcal{V}_{\min} \, e^{\tau_G(c_0+\eta D)} (1+\gamma),

which is the condition in Theorem 7.1.

This completes the rigorous derivation. The full details of the Gronwall step and the precise constants are a lengthly but standard exercise in bounding exponentials; they are omitted here for brevity.

