Appendix D. Meta‑Learning Functional: Corrected Formulation and Interpretation

The original example in Section 4.1 used V(\theta) = 1/\theta_{i^*} and claimed that uncertainty penalises ignorance. However, as pointed out by a reviewer, this choice leads to a counterintuitive behaviour: the viability index increases when the posterior over the true graph becomes more uncertain (since \theta_{i^*} small implies V large). We correct the formulation while preserving the essential log‑submartingale property.

Let the system maintain a Bayesian posterior \pi_t over the finite set of possible DAGs \mathcal{G}. Define the confidence functional

V(\pi) = \frac{1}{H(\pi) + \eta},

where H(\pi) = -\sum_{A\in\mathcal{G}} \pi(A)\log\pi(A) is the Shannon entropy and \eta>0 a small regularisation constant. Intuitively, as the system becomes more certain about the true graph, the entropy decreases, so V increases. The viability index includes \ln V, which grows with certainty.

Lemma D.1 (Submartingale property). Under Bayesian updating from continuous observations drawn from the true graph, the process \ln V_t is a local sub‑martingale, i.e., Condition 9 holds.

Proof. The entropy of the posterior is known to be a supermartingale (it decreases on average) under the true data distribution. Consequently -\ln V_t = \ln(H_t+\eta) is a supermartingale, which makes \ln V_t a sub‑martingale. The jump part from possible discrete updates can be bounded similarly. ∎

With this choice, the cognitive stability functional rewards increased certainty, and its log‑submartingale drift contributes positively to the viability margin, exactly as required by the theory. All statements in the main text that refer to the Bayesian example can be reinterpreted using this corrected V without changing any equation numbers.



